{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZfNdaw3XGjoQLAQk7Hd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, **`divisive_contrast_ideal_alternation`**, and the self-check prints `OK`. That check doesn't cover novelty against the other models, convergence or speed; admission's full fit will test those.\n\n**Hypothesis:** people don't score each sequence on an absolute scale. They compare the two against each other. A sequence's non-randomness is how far its switching rate is from that person's own ideal switching rate, plus how lopsided its heads/tails counts are. The gap between the two sequences is then divided by how non-random the pair looks overall. So a given gap decides the choice strongly when one sequence is close to ideal, and only weakly when both look clearly non-random. The same sequence can therefore be judged differently depending on the sequence beside it, which is what this exploratory slot's brief asked for.\n\n**Model:** it reuses the incumbent's personal ideal switching rate (varying by participant) and a heads/tails imbalance weight constrained to be non-negative. The new part is a gain `beta` and a saturation constant `kappa`:\n`p_left = sigmoid(beta · (dev_b − dev_a) / (kappa + dev_a + dev_b))`.\nA large `kappa` makes this almost the incumbent's absolute-difference model, so the fit shows how strongly people actually compare within the pair.\n\n**Smoke-fit results** (1 chain, 100 draws, 100 tuning steps):\n- ELPD-LOO was −1013.9, against −1008.5 for the current best model. A fit this short can't show whether that gap is real.\n- PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable: 145 of 2,560 trials (5.7%) had a Pareto k above 0.5, over the 1% tolerance. If that holds up in the full fit, the model's ELPD may be untrustworthy.\n- R-hat couldn't be computed from a single chain.\n\n**Risks at admission:**\n- **Novelty:** when `kappa` is large this model predicts almost the same as `ideal_alternation_with_balance`. The last similar candidate, `balanced_personal_ideal_alternation`, was rejected as a near-duplicate of it. If the fitted `kappa` comes out large, this one could be rejected the same way.\n- **Convergence:** `beta` and `kappa` trade off against each other when `kappa` is large, so the full fit may not converge.\n\nThe files are in `iter_2/candidate_0/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2801,"cache_read_input_tokens":53711,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2801,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f4ebb93b-99d6-45bf-8657-cd3d45d02bd2","uuid":"12f6fd34-5e9e-417b-89de-27367ee1ad50","timestamp":"2026-09-30T12:57:50.615Z","request_id":"req_011CfZfNcXB671dbTKGDDe8d"}
